      <span class="abstract-full has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.18294v1-abstract-full" style="display: none;">
        Releasing a neural network checkpoint can leak information about its training data, and existing reconstruction results rely on gradient-descent training. We ask whether the choice of <span class="search-hit mathjax">optimizer</span> affects the ability to recover the training data. Extending work connecting Karush--Kuhn--Tucker conditions to training-data reconstruction, we show that the implicit biases of popular <span class="search-hit mathjax">optimizers</span> (SGD, <span class="search-hit mathjax">Adam</span>, Muon) leave very different fingerprints of the training data. We argue that the key distinction is the \emph{weight-specificity} of these implicit biases, namely how much continuous information about the final weights the <span class="search-hit mathjax">optimizer</span>-dependent target of the stationarity conditions encodes. The limiting stationarity conditions for models trained with SGD and Muon retain enough weight-specific information to reconstruct training examples in practice. For <span class="search-hit mathjax">Adam</span> in the $\ell_\infty$ implicit-bias regime, the <span class="search-hit mathjax">optimizer</span>-dependent part of these conditions encodes only a discrete pattern of maximal coordinates and their signs, and at nondegenerate KKT points the conditions are too few to identify the training set to first order. Experiments on MNIST and CIFAR-10 support these claims: models trained with Muon can yield stronger reconstructions at much lower training cost than models trained with SGD, although this advantage depends on the training regime and architecture, and models trained with <span class="search-hit mathjax">Adam</span> appear consistently more resistant to the evaluated attacks.
